! ===========================================================================
! B7.6 example 2 -- deliberately misaligned A | B, fcc Cu(111)
!                   A NEGATIVE CONTROL. This run is SUPPOSED to warn.
! ===========================================================================
!
! Geometry is identical to ../fccCu111_AA: one active fcc Cu(111) layer
! between two frozen Cu reference regions. The ONLY difference is that this
! input DECLARES the two regions to sit on different absolute energy scales,
!
!     fermi_a = -0.089874     ! the true converged Cu value
!     fermi_b = -0.109874     ! a lie: 20 mRy below it
!
! while both regions are in fact loaded from the SAME converged Cu parameter
! set. So the declared contact potential (20 mRy) and the physical one (zero,
! because the two regions are the same material) disagree by construction.
!
! WHAT THIS EXAMPLE DEMONSTRATES
!
! B7 §1.3 identifies silent misalignment as the highest correctness risk in
! the whole work package: each bulk calculation carries its own potential
! zero, and freezing two of them side by side with no relative shift silently
! imposes "contact potential == 0" whether or not that is true. The run
! converges, looks plausible, and reports a wrong dipole barrier.
!
! G-B7-2 (docs/dev/CONTRACT_FROZEN_REGION.md) decided NOT to defend against
! this by refusing to run on old parameter sets -- backwards compatibility
! won, permanently, and no version stamp exists anywhere. The defence is
! instead the align_regions CONSISTENCY CHECK, which compares the converged
! fixed point against the analytic contact potential E_F(A) - E_F(B).
!
! This example exists to show that that check has teeth. Running it prints,
! every iteration:
!
!   <warning> align_regions: converged alignment shift for region B disagrees
!   with the analytic contact potential E_F(anchor) - E_F(region) by
!   0.020000 Ry, which exceeds the 0.0050 Ry threshold. ...  treat the
!   reported dipole barrier as unvalidated.
!
! 0.020000 Ry is exactly the injected discrepancy, recovered.
!
! WHY THE SOLVER "IGNORES" fermi_b, AND WHY THAT IS CORRECT
!
! fermi_a/fermi_b do NOT set the alignment. They only (i) seed the initial
! guess and (ii) drive this consistency check. The alignment itself is solved
! as an SCF fixed point on the deep-probe residual, precisely because B7 §1.3
! declines to trust a cross-run absolute energy scale.
!
! So here the seeded shift V(B) = +0.020 Ry is driven back toward the value
! the physics actually supports -- near zero, because the two regions really
! are the same material -- and the declared 20 mRy survives only as the
! reported discrepancy. The fixed point is the answer; the declared Fermi
! levels are the check. That is the intended division of labour.
!
! THE ACCEPTED RESIDUAL RISK
!
! The check is SILENT when fermi_a/fermi_b are not both supplied: with no
! declared Fermi levels there is nothing to compare the fixed point against.
! Delete the two entries from &charge below and the warning disappears while
! the run is no less wrong. G-B7-2 accepted this knowingly. Supply
! fermi_a/fermi_b whenever both regions' converged E_F are known.
!
! Runtime: ~15 s per SCF iteration on one core.
! ===========================================================================
&calculation
pre_processing = 'buildinterface'
verbose = T
/
&lattice
rc = 30
alat = 3.614
crystal_sym = 'fcc'
pbc = .true.
n1 = 16
n2 = 16
n3 = 16
wav = 1.41237
nbulk_bulk = 1
nlay = 1
nlay_a = 1
nlay_b = 1
surftype = '1 1 1'
ntype = 3
ct(:) = 3*4.0d0
r2 = 16.0d0
/
&atoms
database = './'
label(1) = 'A'
label(2) = 'B'
label(3) = 'Ac1'
/
&self
nstep = 30
/
&energy
fermi = -0.089874
energy_min = -1.5
energy_max = 2.0
channels_ldos = 2000
fix_fermi = .true.
/
&control
calctype = 'L'
nsp = 1
lld = 150
recur = 'chebyshev'
/
&mix
beta = 0.0020
mixtype = 'broyden'
/
&charge
! ROW counts in the synthetic Madelung stack -- NOT atomic layers, and
! deliberately separate from the &lattice entries above.
!
! LEFT UNSET ON PURPOSE. The active zone must be CENTRED in the stack, or the
! two deep probes sit at very different distances from the active charge and
! the alignment solver reports a nonzero V_B for a physically symmetric cell.
! Unset, build_interface_registry centres it automatically -- (nbas - nlay)/2
! either side. Set these only for a deliberately asymmetric stack.
/
